<!--
  The JFR events that the performance tests' profiled JVMs record in addition to, or instead of, the JDK's "profile"
  configuration. A component's profiling settings list the JFR configurations it records with,
  jfrConfigurations: [profile, pulsar.jfc] by default, which the launcher merges with "jfr configure" in the component's
  image and passes to async-profiler's jfrsync option: a name such as profile is one of the JDK's configurations, and a
  name ending with .jfc is a file of this directory. To record other events or change their settings, edit this file;
  an event that the JVM doesn't have is ignored. For a purpose that needs more events, add a file here and list it in a
  component's jfrConfigurations, as netty-allocations.jfc is.

  The events of Netty's buffer allocators (Netty 4.2: AdaptivePoolingAllocator and PooledByteBufAllocator), as
  Netty's microbench/src/main/resources/Netty Allocator Events.jfc lists them, without their legacy names.
  io.netty.ReturnChunk is ignored until the JVM's Netty version has it.

  The events of every buffer allocation and free, io.netty.AllocateBuffer and io.netty.FreeBuffer, are in
  netty-allocations.jfc instead, since there is an event for every buffer, and JFR can't sample them. In the IoT
  telemetry max-rate scenario, they were about 2.5 events of each kind per message in each profiled component, which
  made a broker's recording 1 GB instead of 10 MB. The chunk events show the one-off buffers without them: a buffer that
  doesn't fit the pooled memory gets an unpooled chunk of its own (io.netty.AllocateChunk with pooled false).
-->
<configuration version="2.0" label="Pulsar"
    description="The events that Pulsar's performance tests add to the JDK's profile configuration">
  <category label="Netty">
    <event name="io.netty.ReallocateBuffer">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">false</setting>
      <setting name="threshold">0 ns</setting>
    </event>
    <event name="io.netty.AllocateChunk">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">false</setting>
      <setting name="threshold">0 ns</setting>
    </event>
    <event name="io.netty.FreeChunk">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">false</setting>
      <setting name="threshold">0 ns</setting>
    </event>
    <event name="io.netty.ReturnChunk">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">false</setting>
      <setting name="threshold">0 ns</setting>
    </event>
  </category>
</configuration>
